Pre-trained networks now circulate as shared artifacts, so a tampered checkpoint on a
popular hub reaches every downstream user. Prior attacks such as StegoNet, EvilModel
and MaleficNet show that float32 weights can hide megabytes of data at negligible
accuracy cost, yet they assume the model is run exactly as published. It rarely is.
Before reaching an edge or medical (IoMT) device a model is quantized to INT8, pruned
and fine-tuned, and we confirm on real networks that these routine steps erase
mantissa-bit payloads entirely. We then show that they need not. We present \sys, a
quantization-aware embedding that writes each payload bit into the INT8 code that
quantization produces, so it survives by construction; placing every weight at a random point inside its code's bin
keeps the mantissa distribution natural and defeats byte-image steganalysis, while
redundant coding carries the payload through pruning and fine-tuning. On a real
ResNet-50 a $3.2$\,MB payload survives INT8 at $99.9999\%$ fidelity; the payload also
survives PyTorch's production INT8 kernel byte-exactly once the embedding matches the
framework's scale convention; on a real
ResNet-56 the attack costs $0.19$ percentage points of accuracy; and against the
released Model X-Ray detector on a diverse model population our payload is
indistinguishable from benign weights ($0.50$, chance), whereas the prior attack is
flagged ($0.81$). Underlying these results is a limit we prove for the function-preserving
neuron-permutation group of convolutional and dense layers: a capacity-robustness-stealth
trilemma in which at most two of the three hold at once, so any stealthy,
quantization-robust, permutation-invariant channel is bounded by
$\sum_\ell k_h^{(\ell)}k_w^{(\ell)}$ bits. The high-capacity payload survives INT8,
pruning and fine-tuning \emph{in the weights}, but a faithful neuron permutation
(function-preserving to $10^{-14}$) erases it; what then survives is only that small
invariant channel, enough to carry a $128$-bit key whose ciphertext is delivered out of
band. We are explicit that under permutation this is survival of a bootstrap key channel,
not of the in-weight payload. We also propose a sub-quantum
residual detector that reliably flags the off-the-shelf attack (ROC-AUC $0.98$--$1.0$
across a narrow and a diverse $3000$-model zoo) where byte-level steganalysis fails, while
showing that an adaptive attacker still reaches chance on a diverse population, so detection
must be backed by the structural bound. Deployment sanitization alone is therefore
incomplete, and we set out what closing the gap requires.
